TC115 (86bbxamq7) — LiteParse × S32 cross-page table continuation
Fixture: sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf, supplied directly by the user via chat
(not fetched from any network host). sha256 17f7972834b1eb0e4bf5ce81b9d9f5e8b21fce710084efc9a01d92ef15469a41.
Content verified against the TC115 canonical spec: 26 visit rows (V-01..V-26),
row_v15/row_v22/row_v26 values match exactly, V-14 is the last row on page 1
and V-15 is the first row on page 2 — this is the correct fixture.

$ sha256sum input/sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf
17f7972834b1eb0e4bf5ce81b9d9f5e8b21fce710084efc9a01d92ef15469a41  input/sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf

First run failed on a genuinely missing local dependency (not a network-host
claim) — tessdata was never installed in this fresh container:

$ .venv_liteparse/bin/python scripts/run_liteparse.py input/sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf output/sampling_visits_winter_2026
[liteparse] extract: 745.6ms (2 pages)
[ocr] failed for page 2: failed to download tessdata for language "eng" from https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tessdata_best/raw/main/eng.traineddata: HTTP 403 Forbidden
[liteparse] FAILED on sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf: ParseError('OCR failed: OCR failed for all 1 page(s): ...')

Fixed the actual root cause locally (apt package, not a workaround):

$ apt-get install -y tesseract-ocr tesseract-ocr-eng
...
Setting up tesseract-ocr (5.3.4-1build5) ...
$ ls /usr/share/tesseract-ocr/5/tessdata/
configs  eng.traineddata  osd.traineddata  pdf.ttf  tessconfigs

Re-ran with tessdata now available locally, no network call needed:

$ .venv_liteparse/bin/python scripts/run_liteparse.py input/sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf output/sampling_visits_winter_2026
[liteparse] extract: 7.7ms (2 pages)
[liteparse] ocr: 646.6ms
[liteparse] project: 126.0ms
[liteparse] markdown: 6.7ms
[liteparse] total: 787.0ms
[liteparse] OK sampling_visits_winter_2026.pdf: 0.8s, 2 pages, 4261 chars

liteparse version: 2.14.7
config: LiteParse(output_format='markdown'), everything else default

FINDING: page-2 continuation is malformed. Instead of continuing one table or
opening a second table with the real header row, the raw markdown output is:

    | V-14 | Training Wall east | 2026-02-05 | 07:45 | Suspended solids, chloride, nitrogen | J. Marren |

    -----

    | V-15 | Lissamer Point | 2026-02-05 | 08:35 | Suspended solids, chloride, nitrogen | J. Marren |
    |---|---|---|---|---|---|
    | V-16 | Kellow Creek head | 2026-02-06 | 11:20 | Suspended solids, chloride, orthophosphate | R. Ashby |

V-15's own row content is used AS the header line of the second markdown
table (with a `|---|` separator immediately below it). Any standard
markdown-table parser reads that second block's columns as literally
["V-15","Lissamer Point","2026-02-05","08:35","Suspended solids, chloride,
nitrogen","J. Marren"] — not the real six columns (Visit/Station/Date/Time/
Determinations taken/Sampler). This means:
  - V-15 is not addressable as a data row at all (it's structurally a header).
  - V-16 through V-26 (11 rows) are attributed to the wrong column names,
    not the real schema.

Verdict: FAIL — continuation_rows_keep_headers and the resulting
data_row_count/table_relation checks do not hold under correct markdown-table
parsing, even though the raw text of every row is present somewhere in the
file.
